# Global Preferences

## Code Style

Primary languages: Python, YAML (Helm/CI), TypeScript, Markdown. When making code changes, always run the project's formatter (e.g., prettier for TS, ruff/black for Python) before committing.

## Version Control

- This repo uses `jj` (Jujutsu), not git. Never use `git stash` - it can revert tracked changes. Use `jj` commands for all VCS operations.
- Before rebuilding or rewriting commits, always check `jj status` for working-copy changes and preserve them explicitly.

## Git Workflow

- When modifying a PR branch, always build on top of existing commits. Never reset the branch to origin/main unless explicitly asked to do so.

## PR Scope Discipline

- Keep each PR focused on a single concern. Do NOT mix unrelated fixes (e.g., pre-existing lint errors, unrelated deployment changes) into a feature PR.
- If you notice unrelated issues while working, surface them to the user as a separate suggestion rather than fixing them inline.
- Never commit or push without explicit user confirmation unless they've said 'push it' for this task.

## CI/CD & GitHub Actions

- When creating dependabot.yml configs, always include grouping strategies and commit-message prefix configuration. Ask for the user's preferred format if not specified.
- For CI workflows on ubuntu-slim runners: Docker-based GitHub Actions are NOT supported. Always use curl/binary installation approaches instead of Docker container actions.

## Code Review / PR Workflow

- Always run an adversarial review (Codex/parallel agents) against your own fix before opening a PR, and verify proposed changes against the real source and prod behavior — proposed fixes have broken prod.

## Communication / Slack

- When drafting Slack messages, default to short, casual tone with no blockquote formatting, and ask for incident/PIP background context up front before writing.

## Issue & PR Conventions

- Do not include fix code in Linear/Jira issue descriptions — describe the approach in prose only.
- Always assign the user (GitHub handle: `HotThoughts`) as assignee on every PR opened (`gh pr create --assignee HotThoughts` or `gh pr edit <num> --add-assignee HotThoughts` for PRs already open).
- PR titles/descriptions: keep them minimal — conventional-commit title (e.g. `fix(values): ...`) and a 2-3 sentence body, no headers/sections. Add `Stacked on #X` when part of a stack.
- When the work relates to a Linear issue, use the issue number as the conventional-commit scope in the PR title (e.g. `fix(DEP-1234): ...`), never in the PR description.

## Memory & Config Scope

- When saving learned context/memory, default to the GLOBAL scope (`~/.claude/`) unless the note is specific to one repo.
- When adding MCP servers, ask the user if they want user-level or project-level scope before registering.

## Testing

- When writing tests for model outputs or numerical comparisons, use generous tolerances (especially for FP16/quantized models) and avoid strict ordering assertions for near-tied scores.
- After every code change that touches handlers, protobuf fields, or tests, run the relevant test suite and type checks locally before pushing; do not push until CI-equivalent checks pass.

